cerned about  democracy was the way in which democracy  arguably stands sort of in opposition to  expertise and the idea of  the most informed people making  decisions which of course as we  understand it today we have a crisis of  so-called expertise and the expert  industrial complex and it brings up a  whole other slate of issues if we were  to go in that direction so to step back  from this and take kind of like a 40,000  foot view what are the most important  and relevant critiques of democracy that  you would make in modern day yeah I  think first of all one of the things  that John Adams insisted on you know he  believed that people should not be in  power forever he believed it was very  important even at the level of who we  elect a congress that they needed to be  returned to the people they needed to be  humbled they needed to stay in touch  with the people he also believed  strongly in the importance of the town  meeting and this is actually comparable  to what Jefferson said when he described  war democracy they they both believed  that there needed to be a local  foundation for democracy that the people  actually knew how to effectively  articulate their demands and John Adams  because in Massachusetts they believed  in education which they didn't in  Virginia because they didn't want to pay  for it yes he believed that from infancy  this is what he heard from emphasis at  essentially people needed to learn how  to deliberate debate and articulate  their views and this came from the local  institution of the Town Meeting his son  John Quincy Adams would carry on that  view when he becomes the ardent  defendant defender of the petition and  petitions were the most widespread way  for for everyone in society to  articulate their grievances and in fact  he defended the right of petition it's  not only for for white men but for  slaves for women and even for the you  know the vilified anti-slavery activists  so this is this is one of the essential  ingredients of John and John Quincy  Adams it gets forgotten and it's because  their New Englanders  that they they still kind of fall into  that camp of believing in local politics  and that's essential to democracy the  other issue that I think is relevant  today is the one that I was getting to  is the as we know we have seen our  executive branch become more powerful  this really begins in Eisenhower's error  this is where the right of executive  privilege was first invented we also see  the increasing power of the executive  and foreign policy and one of the things  this is what they were deeply concerned  with because with John Quincy Adams what  he saw emerging around Andrew Jackson  was very much this cult the what we  refer to as the warrior cult of  popularity and the fact that he saw  people being obsequious this is his  words obsequious to executive power and  this is one of the dangers I mean in a  sense it's one of those basic principles  we always go back to but John and John  Quincy Adams believe that you need it  each branch of government to be able to  check the power of other branches of  government because they're all prone to  abuses and so what I mean along those  lines what changes what what are the the  specific changes that could be made in  order to address these issues that have  developed yeah well I think one of the  key issues I've already mentioned is the  problem of regulating the amount of  money that controls politics today we  also need to think about the the  problems that we know we all know about  and many people are writing about right  now is is the damage done through the  internet the way and this is why I  talked about why we have this kind of  virtual cult of personality the fact  that so much of people's information  today comes through the internet the  fact that there's not only do we have  the abuse of elections that can come  through the internet but people in a  sense are fed things that as we know are  untrue are lies this is what John Adams  referred to as quackery and I think this  is the problem and this gets back to  your point that yes we need an educated  citizenry but not just educated in a  sense that it's it's only the college  elites or the professional elites have  to control government it gets back to  this idea of making sure that people  through the town meeting can make their  representatives accountable you remember  remember when several politicians  actually had town meetings who had never  had them before and they suddenly had to  answer questions that the media weren't  asking them yeah and I think this is  this is one of the issues we have to  find a way to address briefly what is  the degree to which the sort of  celebrity worship type culture that we  have today and that in great part is at  least partially responsible for Donald  Trump becoming president of the United  States how much of an issue was that in  the time of the atom's as in earlier  times in the United States sort of  political of infrastructure yeah I know  we considered clearly identified Donald  Trump's rise through his role in reality  TV but it's not new essentially this is  one of the things we highlight I mean  the first time the John Adams sees the  cult of personality takes shape is when  he is in France and watching Benjamin  Franklin turned into the first American  rock star he was adored by the French  people and he played the role he was  always on a stage and he used that as  part of his you know diplomatic  gamesmanship then we have George  Washington you know and this is where  the danger is with the executive branch  because part of where the invented  powers of the executive branch come from  barring from monarchy monarchy borrowing  from the traditions of royalty this is  where executive privilege actually comes  from and in addition to that if one of  the things John Adams said about  Washington is the idea that you know  Washington was successful because of  invented stories 